# NCCL all-reduce microbenchmark on the real ring — zero install, no serving.
#
# Purpose: measure the latency and the bus bandwidth of a 4-rank ring all-reduce at the
# message sizes vLLM actually issues (W1: 32 KB decode steps in RING/LL, 20-67 MB prefill
# chunks in SIMPLE), under EXACTLY the production NCCL environment. This overlay reuses
# launcher/launch-glm53-tp4.sh unchanged, so the container gets the patched libnccl via
# LD_PRELOAD, NCCL_ALGO=Ring, 4 channels, both RoCE HCAs and the same --master-addr; only
# the entrypoint changes, from vllm-serve to /bench/entry.sh.
#
# *** MUST be run with the production stack DOWN. *** Safe by construction: the container
# name differs, so it can never collide with glm53_fp8_dflash_tp4 — but both stacks would
# fight over the GPUs and over the fabric, and the numbers would be meaningless.
#
# CONTAINER EXCEPTION. experiments/README.md says overlays never override CONTAINER, so
# that `tp4ctl status`/`down` without TP4_ENV always see the single production stack. This
# is the one deliberate exception: this overlay NEVER serves anything (no API port is
# opened, the process exits on its own after a few minutes), so it must not be reachable
# by the production control path at all. Consequences, and they are mandatory:
#   - NEVER run `tp4ctl up`, `tp4ctl restart` or the autostart unit with this TP4_ENV;
#   - drive it ONLY through scripts/nccl-bench.sh, which refuses to start while the
#     production container is up and removes glm53_ncclbench on every node at the end;
#   - `tp4ctl down` without TP4_ENV does NOT clean this container up.
#
# The launcher still runs its normal preflight (model dir, patched NCCL, drafter, indexer
# patch), `docker rm -f $CONTAINER`, the sysctl/drop_caches ritual and the rank<->IP check.
# All of that is correct and harmless with the stack down; the files it checks are the
# production ones and are present on every node.
#
# NOTE on the bind mount path: the source of every `-v` pair in EXTRA_DOCKER_ENV is
# expanded by the launcher, on the node, when it starts with $HOME (or ~) — see
# expand_home() in launch-glm53-tp4.sh. Keep the value SINGLE-quoted so $HOME survives
# until then: cluster.env is also sourced on the workstation (tp4ctl, deploy.sh).
#
# Deploy: TP4_ENV=experiments/2026-09-04-ncclbench.env ./scripts/deploy.sh
#         (also pushes node/nccl-bench/* to ~/tp4/nccl-bench/)
# Run:    ./scripts/nccl-bench.sh
# TP4_NO_SERVE=1 is the machine-checkable half of the rule above: tp4ctl refuses `up` and
# `restart` when it is set, so the CONTAINER exception cannot be driven by the serving path
# by mistake. The launcher and scripts/nccl-bench.sh ignore it; autostart never sets TP4_ENV.
TP4_NO_SERVE=1
CONTAINER=glm53_ncclbench
EXTRA_DOCKER_ENV='--entrypoint /bench/entry.sh -v $HOME/tp4/nccl-bench:/bench:ro'
